body: "## Problem\n\n`decideDailyRelease` (`daily-release.ts`, v2026.11.14 / 7687a35) resumes any untagged `package.json` version as `pending`:\n\n```ts\nconst pending = current && /^\\d{4}\\.\\d{1,2}\\.\\d{1,2}(-\\d+)?$/.test(current)\n  && !input.releaseTags.some(tag => releaseCoordinate(tag) === releaseCoordinate(current)) ? current : undefined;\nconst version = input.packageVersion ?? pending ?? releaseVersion(input.today);\n```\n\nThat rule lets a release that merged but was never tagged resume after a day rollover. Packages whose first npm version was published by hand (no git tag, no provenance) also look exactly like that. prepare then builds a candidate for a version npm already holds, the release PR merges, and the release step must refuse it:\n\n> `<pkg>@<version> exists on the registry without matching commit and artifact provenance ... Refusing to tag and release around it`\n\nThe next prepare picks the same untagged version again, so these packages can never release through the flow.\n\n## Evidence (2026-11-16)\n\n| Package | package.json / candidate on main | npm latest | git tags |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| pm-review | 2026.10.12 (release PR #90 merged 16:45Z) | 2026.10.12 | 0 |\n| pm-brand | 2026.10.17 (#53 merged 16:34Z) | 2026.10.17 | 0 |\n| pm-rlm | 2026.10.18 | 2026.10.18 | 0 |\n| pm-glossary | 2026.11.5 (#24 merged 17:05Z) | 2026.11.5 | 0 |\n| pm-reduce, pm-screen | 2026.11.6 | 2026.11.6 | 0 |\n| pm-unit, pm-hypothesis, pm-http | untagged = npm latest (next in line) | same | 0 |\n\nThe 11-15 pm-review and pm-rlm prepares also targeted 2026.10.12 and 2026.10.18.\n\n## Proposal\n\n- In prepare's `decide` step, before treating an untagged version as `pending`, check the registry (`npm view <name>@<version> dist --json`). If that version exists, it is not a resumable release, so fall through to `releaseVersion(today)`. Unit-test `decideDailyRelease` with a new `registryHasCurrentVersion` input for both cases.\n- In reconcile/release, when the merged candidate's version already exists on npm without this flow's provenance, retire the candidate (the same path as `retire-unpublished`) and let the next prepare cut today's version. Today's behaviour, a red run on every reconcile, recovers nothing.\n- Optional: record the hand-published first version as a GitHub release or tag without re-publishing, so the changelog and release list line up with npm.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A repository whose untagged `package.json` version already exists on npm gets a candidate for today's date, not the old version.\n- A truly pending (merged, untagged, not yet on npm) version still resumes after a rollover.\n- An already-merged stale candidate is retired with a clear notice instead of failing every hourly reconcile.\n\n\n---\n\n### GitHub comments (1)\n\n> **@unbraind** (2026-11-16T19:48:20Z)\n>\n> Recovery evidence for #291 at `063e79c`: I checked exact npm version timestamps and candidate runs for Review #90, RLM #77, Case #18, Screen #15, Glossary #24, and Reduce #12. All six npm coordinates existed before their respective trusted preparation runs; the published integrity differs from the candidate and there is still no matching tag or GitHub release. The first five have the exact candidate tree and single parent at merge. This does not yet establish all protection/configuration prerequisites.\n> \n> [Reduce #12](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-reduce/pull/12) is an exception: candidate `e798d6d` recorded parent `16421b6` and tree `13e2fdb`; merge [`4eca4d7`](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-reduce/commit/4eca4d796f11904ca729c39df0dfc22d365d7148) has parent `6e86d7d` and tree `4943624`. The original preparation artifact remains available. The added `!changedSource` guard correctly refuses automatic retirement for this receipt. Please track it as a separate operator-reviewed recovery case rather than treating successful rollout of #291 as recovery of all six or loosening that guard. This was a read-only comparison; no retirement, tag or release attempted."
